  "prediction": "The report outlines the process for overriding a presidential veto in the United States Congress. When the President vetoes a bill, both the House of Representatives and the Senate have the power to override the veto with a two-thirds vote in each chamber. The report details the steps involved in this process, including the receipt of the veto message, its publication in the Senate and House journals, and the debate and vote on the question of overriding the veto. The report also notes that if the Senate fails to override a veto of a Senate-originated bill, it informs the House and the bill does not reach a vote in the House. If the Senate overrides a veto of a Senate-originated bill or the House overrides a veto of a House-originated bill, the bill becomes law. The report emphasizes that the process requires a supermajority vote and careful consideration by members of Congress.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the process for overriding a presidential veto in the United States Congress. When the President vetoes a bill, both the House of Representatives and the Senate have the power to override the veto with a two-thirds vote in each chamber. The report details the steps involved in this process, including the receipt of the veto message, its publication in the Senate and House journals, and the debate and vote on the question of overriding the veto. The report also notes that if the Senate fails to override a veto of a Senate-originated bill, it informs the House and the bill does not reach a vote in the House. If the Senate overrides a veto of a Senate-originated bill or the House overrides a veto of a House-originated bill, the bill becomes law. The report emphasizes that the process requires a supermajority vote and careful consideration by members of Congress.",
  "score": 31.645569124178824,
  "answers": [
    "A bill or joint resolution that has been vetoed by the President can become law if two-thirds of the Members voting in the House and the Senate each agree to pass it over the President's objection. The chambers act sequentially on vetoed measures: The House acts first on House-originated measures (H.R. and H.J. Res.), and the Senate acts first on Senate-originated measures (S. and S.J. Res.). If the first-acting chamber fails to override the veto, the other chamber cannot consider it. The House typically considers the question of overriding a presidential veto under the hour rule, with time customarily controlled and allocated by the chair and ranking member of the committee with jurisdiction over the bill. The Senate usually considers the question of overriding a veto under the terms of a unanimous consent agreement."
  ],
